'''Alex Bores''' (born November 2, 1990)<ref>{{Cite web |title=New York State Assemblymember Alex Bores - Biography {{!}} LegiStorm |url=https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/442541/Alexander_Bores.html |access-date=2026-06-22 |website=www.legistorm.com |language=en}}</ref> is an American politician serving in the [[New York State Assembly]] since 2023. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he represents the [[New York's 73rd State Assembly district|73rd district]] in Manhattan's [[Upper East Side]].

Before entering politics, Bores worked as a [[Data science|data scientist]] and technology consultant. He earned a bachelor's degree from [[Cornell University]] and a master's degree in computer science from the [[Georgia Tech|Georgia Institute of Technology]].

His work in office has included [[artificial intelligence policy]], including co-authoring the [[Responsible AI Safety and Education Act]] (RAISE Act). He was a Democratic candidate in the [[2026 United States House of Representatives elections in New York#District 12|2026]] election for [[New York's 12th congressional district]].

== Early life and education ==
Bores was born in [[Manhattan]], New York City.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Johnson |first=Atticus |date=December 4, 2025 |title=NY Assemblyman Alex Bores ’13, Architect of AI Regulation Bill, Mounts Run for Congress in Manhattan |url=https://www.cornellsun.com/article/2025/12/ny-assemblyman-alex-bores-13-architect-of-ai-regulation-bill-mounts-run-for-congress-in-manhattan |access-date=June 21, 2026 |work=The Cornell Daily Sun}}</ref> He is a fifth-generation New Yorker.<ref name=":6">{{Cite news |last=Nichols |first=John |date=2026-06-19 |title=How a New York Primary Wound Up at the Center of the AI Storm |url=https://www.thenation.com/article/politics/alex-bores-ai-new-york-12-primary/ |access-date=2026-06-21 |language=en-US |issn=0027-8378|work=The Nation}}</ref> His father was a union member,<ref name="JI-2026-05-14">{{Cite web |title=Alex Bores' father, a key ally in his son's House campaign, celebrated Israeli deaths, equated Zionists with Nazis |url=https://jewishinsider.com/2026/05/alex-bores-father-william-bores-house-campaign-israel/ |access-date=June 24, 2026 |website=Jewish Insider |date=May 14, 2026 |last=Bredderman |first=Will}}</ref> who worked in television sports production,<ref name="nyt">{{Cite web |title=Ezra Klein Podcast: Alex Bores |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/21/opinion/ezra-klein-podcast-alex-bores.html |access-date=May 27, 2026 |website=The New York Times |date=April 21, 2026}}</ref> and his mother was a television news writer.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Kayatt |first=Arlene |date=2024-01-07 |title=Lori Bores, Mom of Assemblyman, Is Longtime Community Activist Herself |url=https://www.ourtownny.com/voices/lori-bores-mom-of-assemblyman-is-longtime-community-activist-herself-XA3020725 |access-date=2026-06-21 |website=www.ourtownny.com |language=en-US}}</ref> He grew up on the [[Upper East Side]] and attended [[Hunter College High School]].<ref name="ourtown">{{Cite web |last=Gruskin |first=Abigail |date=2022-03-16 |title=Alex Bores Plans to Tackle Politics Like He’s Tackled Tech — With Results |url=https://www.ourtownny.com/news/alex-bores-plans-to-tackle-politics-like-he-s-tackled-tech-with-results-BE1996254 |access-date=2026-04-03 |website=www.ourtownny.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

He earned a bachelor's degree in [[industrial relations]] and economics from [[Cornell University]].<ref name=":0">{{cite news |last1=Scola |first1=Nancy |title=The Campaign to Take Down Alex Bores Is Just the Beginning |url=https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2026/03/06/alex-bores-ai-leading-the-future-anthropic-00797055 |access-date=March 7, 2026 |publisher=[[Politico]] |date=March 6, 2026 |archive-date=March 11, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260311011254/https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2026/03/06/alex-bores-ai-leading-the-future-anthropic-00797055 |url-status=live }}</ref> While at Cornell, Bores was elected to the university's [[Governing boards of colleges and universities in the United States|board of trustees]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Oreskes |first=Benjamin |date=2026-06-06 |title=A.I. Companies Don’t Know What to Do With Alex Bores |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/06/nyregion/alex-bores-primary-ai-palantir.html |access-date=2026-06-22 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> He later went to [[Georgia Tech]], where he earned a master's degree in computer science with a specialization in [[machine learning]].<ref name=":6" />

== Early career ==
From 2008 to 2009, Bores served as a constituent services representative for City Councilwoman [[Jessica Lappin]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Garber |first=Nick |date=June 16, 2022 |title=UES 73rd District Assembly Race 2022: Alex Bores Profile |url=https://patch.com/new-york/upper-east-side-nyc/ues-73rd-district-assembly-race-2022-alex-bores-profile |access-date=March 30, 2023 |website=Upper East Side, NY Patch |language=en |archive-date=March 30, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230330033522/https://patch.com/new-york/upper-east-side-nyc/ues-73rd-district-assembly-race-2022-alex-bores-profile |url-status=live }}</ref> He worked as a consultant for [[Cornerstone Research]] from 2013 to 2014.

Bores joined [[Palantir]] in 2014, where he worked as a data scientist, project lead, enterprise lead, and U.S. government lead.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Blake |first=Wendy |date=March 4, 2022 |title=One-time Exec at Controversial Tech Giant Enters Dem Assembly Race, Wants to Use Cyber Savvy to Protect NYC |url=https://eastsidefeed.com/politics/one-time-exec-at-controversial-tech-giant-enters-dem-assembly-race-wants-to-use-cyber-savvy-to-protect-nyc/ |access-date=March 30, 2023 |website=East Side Feed |language=en-US}}</ref> At Palantir, he worked with the [[United States Department of Justice]] to investigate pharmacies overprescribing opioids.<ref name="nyt" /><ref name="oreskes-nyt" /> He also worked with other government agencies including the [[CDC|Center for Disease Control]] and the [[Department of Veteran Affairs]].<ref name="nyt" /> Bores left Palantir in 2019, saying he disagreed with its decision to renew its contract with [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement|U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE).<ref>{{Cite news |last=Zeff |first=Maxwell |date=November 21, 2025 |title=A $100 Million AI Super PAC Targeted New York Democrat Alex Bores. He Thinks It Backfired |url=https://www.wired.com/story/alex-bores-andreessen-horowitz-super-pac-ai-regulation-new-york/ |access-date=January 14, 2026 |work=Wired |language=en-US |issn=1059-1028 |archive-date=January 15, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260115041340/https://www.wired.com/story/alex-bores-andreessen-horowitz-super-pac-ai-regulation-new-york/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="CSNY-Bores-Palantir">{{cite news |last=McDonough |first=Annie |title=Alex Bores' work at Palantir complicates his anti-ICE stance |url=https://www.cityandstateny.com/politics/2026/01/alex-bores-work-palantir-complicates-his-anti-ice-stance/410813/ |work=City & State New York |date=January 20, 2026 |access-date=June 2, 2026 |archive-date=June 8, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260608223046/https://www.cityandstateny.com/politics/2026/01/alex-bores-work-palantir-complicates-his-anti-ice-stance/410813/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=McDonough|first=Annie|title=Bores zeros in on Lasher|work=City & State NY|date=January 21, 2026|url=https://www.cityandstateny.com/politics/2026/01/bores-zeros-in-lasher/410846/}}</ref> [[Bloomberg News]] later reported conflicting accounts of the circumstances surrounding his departure, which Bores disputed.<ref name="bloomberg">{{Cite web |title=NY Congressional Candidate Faced Palantir Sexual Comments Claim |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-02-13/ny-congressional-candidate-faced-palantir-sexual-comments-claim |access-date=May 27, 2026 |website=Bloomberg |date=February 13, 2026 |last=Nahmias |first=Laura}}</ref><ref name="nyt" />

From 2019 to 2020, Bores was the head of the commercial division and acting general manager of Merlon Intelligence,<ref name=":1" /> an anti-[[money laundering]] risk management platform.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mannes |first=John |date=2017-05-23 |title=Merlon Intelligence raises $7.65 million in seed financing to combat money laundering |url=https://techcrunch.com/2017/05/23/merlon-intelligence-raises-7-65-million-in-seed-financing-to-combat-money-laundering/ |access-date=2026-06-19 |website=TechCrunch |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="CSNY-Bores-Palantir" /> From 2020 to 2022, he served as the head of [[customer success]] and president of transportation practice at Promise.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Alex Bores '13 |url=https://www.ilr.cornell.edu/75/our-stories/alex-bores-13 |access-date=March 30, 2023 |website=Cornell University School of Industrial and Labor Relations |date=January 25, 2021 |language=en}}</ref>

== New York State Assembly ==
In March 2022, Bores entered the race for [[New York State Assembly]] in District 73 with a platform including affordability and renewable energy. He was elected in November 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New York State Assembly - District 73 Election Results |url=https://www.app.com/elections/results/race/2022-11-08-state_house-NY-39892/ |access-date=2026-04-03 |website=Asbury Park Press |language=en}}</ref>

Bores co-authored the [[Responsible AI Safety and Education Act]] (RAISE Act), an [[AI safety]] bill that passed the New York State Senate and Assembly in June 2025,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Field |first=Hayden |date=December 12, 2025 |title=Parents call for New York governor to sign landmark AI safety bill |url=https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/844062/parents-call-for-new-york-governor-to-sign-landmark-ai-safety-bill |access-date=January 14, 2026 |website=The Verge |language=en-US |archive-date=January 3, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260103022044/https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/844062/parents-call-for-new-york-governor-to-sign-landmark-ai-safety-bill |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Kramer |first1=Marcia |last2=Capellini |first2=Jeff |date=2026-05-17 |title=Alex Bores discusses his candidacy for Rep. Jerry Nadler's seat in the 12th District |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/newyork/news/alex-bores-artificial-intelligence-12th-congressional-district/ |access-date=2026-06-25 |website=CBS New York |language=en-US}}</ref>  with ''[[Politico]]'' saying that it was "widely regarded as the furthest-reaching in the country."<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Field |first=Hayden |date=December 23, 2025 |title=New York’s landmark AI safety bill was defanged — and universities were part of the push against it |url=https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/849293/ai-alliance-universities-colleges-funding-ad-campaign-against-raise-act |access-date=January 14, 2026 |website=The Verge |language=en-US}}</ref> The bill was significantly weakened by Governor [[Kathy Hochul]] before she signed it into law in December 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lewis |first=Rebecca C. |date=2025-12-20 |title=Hochul signs watered down AI regs, but lawmakers still got some wins |url=https://www.cityandstateny.com/policy/2025/12/hochul-signs-watered-down-ai-regs-lawmakers-still-got-some-wins/410328/ |access-date=2026-04-03 |website=City & State NY |language=en |archive-date=April 20, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260420122723/https://www.cityandstateny.com/policy/2025/12/hochul-signs-watered-down-ai-regs-lawmakers-still-got-some-wins/410328/ |url-status=live }}</ref> ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' cited his role in co-sponsoring the RAISE Act when naming him to its 2025 [[Time 100 AI]] list.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chow |first=Andrew R. |date=August 26, 2025 |title=Alex Bores: The 100 Most Influential People in AI 2025 |url=https://time.com/collections/time100-ai-2025/7305812/alex-bores/ |access-date=January 14, 2026 |website=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]}}</ref>

In May 2026, shortly after the [[Second presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration]] announced its [[Anti-Weaponization Fund]] intended to compensate individuals who claim that the DOJ had been weaponized against them, Bores began working on a bill titled the "Anti-Insurrectionist Act". In a draft memo to the State Assembly, the bill was described as ensuring that “no resident of this State is enriched by what is, in substance, a publicly-funded political payout negotiated between the President and his own Administration.”<ref>{{Cite web |last=Siemaszko |first=Corky |date=May 27, 2026 |title=Proposed bill would tax New Yorkers who tap ‘anti-weaponization’ fund at 100% |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/proposed-bill-tax-new-yorkers-tap-anti-weaponization-fund-100-rcna347133 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260528023429/https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/proposed-bill-tax-new-yorkers-tap-anti-weaponization-fund-100-rcna347133 |archive-date=May 28, 2026 |access-date=May 28, 2026 |website=[[NBC News]] |language=en}}</ref>

Bores co-sponsored a bill in the New York state legislature that would impose a one-year moratorium on new [[Data center|data centers]] in the state.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last1=Hashim |first1=Shakeel |last2=Irwin |first2=Veronica |last3=Ford |first3=Celia |date=2026-06-05 |title=The best AI bill yet may not get far |url=https://www.transformernews.ai/p/the-best-ai-bill-yet-may-not-get-obernolte-trahan-gaaia |access-date=2026-06-10 |website=www.transformernews.ai |language=en |archive-date=June 10, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260610014556/https://www.transformernews.ai/p/the-best-ai-bill-yet-may-not-get-obernolte-trahan-gaaia |url-status=live }}</ref> It passed the legislature on June 4 but has not been signed by Hochul.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dalban |first=Lauren |date=2026-06-05 |title=New York State Gets One Step Closer to a Data Center Moratorium |url=https://insideclimatenews.org/news/04062026/new-york-data-center-moratorium-bill/ |access-date=2026-06-10 |website=Inside Climate News |language=en-US |archive-date=June 10, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260610011845/https://insideclimatenews.org/news/04062026/new-york-data-center-moratorium-bill/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The legislature also passed a [[biosecurity]] bill co-sponsored by Bores that would require pathogen screening for [[artificial gene synthesis]].<ref name=":2" />

