In October 2025, Bores announced his candidacy for [[NY-12|New York's 12th district]] in the [[U.S. House of Representatives]] to succeed [[Jerry Nadler]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |title=Manhattan Assemblyman Joins Primary to Succeed Nadler in Congress |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/20/nyregion/alex-bores-ny-congress-primary.html |access-date=October 20, 2025 |website=The New York Times |last=King |first=Maya |date=October 20, 2025 |language=en |archive-date=October 20, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251020124710/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/20/nyregion/alex-bores-ny-congress-primary.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He said he entered the race due to the effects of rapidly advancing technology on American democracy and highlighted Donald Trump's close relationships with tech executives.<ref name=":3" />

The campaign attracted national attention because of its connection to debates over [[artificial intelligence regulation]]. Bores's campaign was targeted by [[Attack ad|attack advertising]] from the [[OpenAI]]-aligned [[super PAC]] network [[Leading the Future]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gold |first=Hadas |date=December 10, 2025 |title=Pro-AI super PAC launches first candidate ads |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/10/tech/pro-ai-super-pac-launches-first-candidate-ads |access-date=December 19, 2025 |website=CNN |language=en |archive-date=December 21, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251221191010/https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/10/tech/pro-ai-super-pac-launches-first-candidate-ads |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Goldberg |first=Michelle |date=February 23, 2026 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/23/opinion/alex-bores-ai-democrats.html |title=A Congressional Candidate Feared by the Tech Oligarchs |work=The New York Times |access-date=February 24, 2026}}</ref> By June 2026, the group had spent more than $7.6 million in opposing his candidacy.<ref name=":4" /> ''Politico'' described the super PAC's tactic as "beat[ing] up on Bores so badly that when the idea of regulating AI development comes up, other politicians run the other direction."<ref name=":0" /> ''Transformer'' described the race as a "microcosm" of a nationwide political-spending battle over AI regulation due to the involvement of "dueling AI-focused super PACs."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Irwin |first=Veronica |date=2026-03-10 |title=Both sides claim the lead in AI's high-stakes midterm race |url=https://www.transformernews.ai/p/alex-bores-ny-12-polling-jack-schlossberg-public-first-leading-the-future-micah-lasher-new-york |access-date=2026-04-02 |website=www.transformernews.ai |language=en |archive-date=April 10, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260410160833/https://www.transformernews.ai/p/alex-bores-ny-12-polling-jack-schlossberg-public-first-leading-the-future-micah-lasher-new-york |url-status=live }}</ref>

Bores's candidacy was supported by Jobs and Democracy PAC, a super PAC aligned with the nonprofit [[Public First Action]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wilkins |first=Emily |date=2026-02-19 |title=Dueling PACs take center stage in midterm elections over AI regulation |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2026/02/19/dueling-pacs-take-center-stage-in-midterm-elections-over-ai-regulation.html |access-date=2026-06-10 |website=CNBC |language=en |archive-date=March 4, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260304122813/https://www.cnbc.com/2026/02/19/dueling-pacs-take-center-stage-in-midterm-elections-over-ai-regulation.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Public First Action received $20 million from the AI company [[Anthropic]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Curi |first=Maria |date=2026-02-12 |title=Anthropic pours $20 million into AI policy fight |url=https://www.axios.com/2026/02/12/anthropic-millions-ai-policy-fight |access-date=2026-03-05 |website=Axios |language=en |archive-date=May 19, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260519012025/https://www.axios.com/2026/02/12/anthropic-millions-ai-policy-fight |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Kang |first=Cecilia |date=2026-02-23 |title=Backed by Anthropic, a Super PAC Group Begins an Ad Blitz in Support of A.I. Regulation |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/23/technology/ai-pac-ad-blitz.html |access-date=2026-03-05 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> According to the company, the funds could not be used for federal election activity.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tiku |first=Nitasha |last2=Duncan |first2=Ian |last3=Vynck |first3=Gerrit De |date=2026-05-23 |title=‘It’s called winning’: Why a tech industry super PAC is running ads about ICE |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2026/05/23/ai-industry-super-pacs-are-intervening-midterm-congressional-races/ |access-date=2026-06-13 |work=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286 |archive-date=May 27, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260527055220/https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2026/05/23/ai-industry-super-pacs-are-intervening-midterm-congressional-races/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Support for Bores's campaign also came from DREAM NYC, a super PAC whose only disclosed donor was an employee of Anthropic who donated $50,000.<ref name="politico-pac">{{Cite web |first1=Chris |last1=Sommerfeldt |first2=Emily |last2=Ngo |first3=Nick |last3=Reisman |first4=Jason |last4=Beeferman |title=The Alex Bores Campaign's PAC Overlap |url=https://www.politico.com/newsletters/new-york-playbook/2026/02/10/the-alex-bores-campaigns-pac-overlap-00772733 |access-date=May 27, 2026 |website=Politico |date=February 10, 2026}}</ref> Additionally, [[Ripple Labs|Ripple]] co-founder [[Chris Larsen]] committed $3.5 million to Bores's campaign, aiming to provide a "counterweight" to the attack advertising.<ref name="nyt-larsen">{{Cite web |last1=Goldmacher |first1=Shane |last2=Fandos |first2=Nicholas |title=A Midterm Clash of A.I. Giants Escalates as a Tech Billionaire Jumps In |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/01/us/politics/alex-bores-chris-larsen-open-ai-jack-schlossberg.html |access-date=May 27, 2026 |website=The New York Times |date=May 1, 2026}}</ref><ref name="politico">{{Cite web |title=Crypto Billionaire Puts $3.5 Million Behind Alex Bores in New York |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/05/01/crypto-billionaire-puts-3-5-million-behind-alex-bores-in-new-york-00902968 |access-date=May 27, 2026 |website=Politico |date=May 1, 2026 |last=Gardiner |first1=Dustin |last2=Fernandez |first2=Madison}}</ref> The campaign was also supported by a new super PAC, Guardrails Alliance, funded by the [[American Association of University Professors]], the [[American Federation of Teachers]], the [[Working Families Party]], and other groups, with a similar goal of counterbalancing the attack spending.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last=Wimbish |first=Whitney Curry |date=2026-06-19 |title=Tech Industry Defector in NY-12 Gets Boost From New Super PAC |url=https://prospect.org/2026/06/19/tech-industry-ny-12-artificial-intelligence-super-pac-alex-bores/ |access-date=2026-06-20 |website=The American Prospect |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Isaac |first=Mike |last2=Schleifer |first2=Theodore |date=2026-06-18 |title=New Super PAC Aims to Rally Tech Workers to Help Limit A.I. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/18/technology/ai-super-pac-guardrails-alliance.html |access-date=2026-06-22 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> According to ''The New York Times'', a total of $6.5 million originating from four different super PACs was spent supporting Bores or attacking his opponents.<ref name="oreskes-nyt">{{cite news |last=Oreskes |first=Benjamin |title=A.I. Companies Don't Know What to Do With Alex Bores |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/06/nyregion/alex-bores-primary-ai-palantir.html |work=The New York Times |date=June 6, 2026 |access-date=June 13, 2026}}</ref>

As of February 2026, the majority of contributions to Bores's campaign had come from outside of New York City, with 12 percent originating from within the congressional district.<ref name="citystate">{{Cite web |last=Thomas |first=Grace |title=Only 12% of Alex Bores' donations come from NY-12 |url=https://www.cityandstateny.com/politics/2026/02/only-12-alex-bores-donations-come-ny-12/411515/ |access-date=May 27, 2026 |website=City & State New York |date=February 19, 2026 |archive-date=May 27, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260527043620/https://www.cityandstateny.com/politics/2026/02/only-12-alex-bores-donations-come-ny-12/411515/ |url-status=live }}</ref> By June 2026, about $12 million in [[independent expenditures]] had been spent to support and oppose his campaign.<ref name="oreskes-nyt"/> In an article published in May 2026, ''[[The New Yorker]]'' described Bores's run as "a proxy battle between [[OpenAI]] and [[Anthropic]]."<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Lewis-Kraus |first=Gideon |date=May 7, 2026 |title=How a Congressional Primary Became a Proxy Battle Over A.I. |magazine=The New Yorker |url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/our-local-correspondents/how-a-congressional-primary-became-a-proxy-battle-over-ai |access-date=May 29, 2026}}</ref>

Bores finished second in the primary election and was defeated by [[Micah Lasher]]. Other Democratic candidates for the seat included [[Jack Schlossberg]] and [[George Conway]], among others.<ref>{{cite news |last=Fandos |first=Nicholas |date=June 23, 2026 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/23/nyregion/ny12-nadler-primary-micah-lasher.html |title=Micah Lasher Wins Manhattan Primary, Defeating Kennedy Heir and Others |work=The New York Times |access-date=June 24, 2026}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Bores is married to Darya Moldavskaya.<ref name=":3" /> They live on the [[Upper East Side]] of Manhattan<ref>{{Cite web |title=Alex Bores - Assembly District 73 {{!}}Assembly Member Directory {{!}} New York State Assembly |url=https://nyassembly.gov/mem/Alex-Bores |access-date=2026-04-02 |website=nyassembly.gov |archive-date=March 12, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260312020433/https://www.nyassembly.gov/mem/Alex-Bores/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and have one son.<ref name=":3" />

